The expected-score function follows Mark Glickman's [Glicko-2 specification](https://glicko.net/glicko/glicko2.pdf). The recent-performance likelihood and its regularization are choices made in this note; they are not represented as the official Glicko-2 estimator.

## 12. Changes from the submitted note

The main changes are the explicit M/A/O/R distinctions; removal of unsupported peak and casual-game signs; a precise performance estimator; strictly pre-game all-source histories within speed; reconstructed rather than purportedly observed record crossings; test-specific nuisance controls; five new primary tests with separate equivalence inference; formal state contrasts; and explicit preservation of prior/frozen external evidence. The original six-test family is not carried forward because T6 is governed by separate work and includes already observed contextual evidence.

This version fixes substantive specifications before the new coefficients are inspected. Any later substantive change must receive a dated amendment explaining what evidence was already known. Resolving physical column mappings or verified file locations without changing the logical definitions is recorded in the construction receipt.

**Specification validation:** the payoff identity, squared-error comparison, 0.75 ordinal threshold and crossing-indicator equivalence were checked numerically over 100,000 synthetic cases with seed 20260923. The prescribed performance estimator gave finite unique solutions in synthetic all-win, all-loss and all-draw histories. The nine-term and 19-term nuisance bases and document cross-references were checked. These checks validate algebra and specification consistency; they are not empirical estimates or production-code validation.
